Discourse markers (DMs) indicate the semantic and pragmatic connections between clauses or sentences. Although recent research has primarily focused on classifying DMs between sentence pairs—often to enhance performance in sentence classification tasks—little work has investigated how this category of language is learned or acquired. This paper adopts a linguistic standpoint to examine recent research on detecting and predicting DMs between sentence pairs. We highlight the distinctions between detection and prediction methods and explore the implications of these techniques for representing the relation between sentences. Additionally, we consider alternative methodologies for predicting discursive semantic relations, proposing a novel framework for this research. Finally, after reviewing existing resources related to DMs and discourse relations, we outline a plan for creating a new dataset that captures both the category of specific markers and their associated discourse relations. This proposed resource will facilitate research into discursive semantic relations and facilitate natural language processing tasks involving DMs. This article is part of the theme issue ' Challenges and opportunities in computational linguistics'.

